ose of the product.\n34-\n35-## How the cycle differs from 495\n36-\n37-The cycle and 495 answer the same question, how to accept a change an agent made, and they do not\n38-answer it the same way. Each difference below is one of three kinds:\n39-- **interim**: it goes away once 495 drives its own changes;\n40-- **under trial**: a different choice that the laboratory weighs against 495's;\n41-- **deliberate**: the cycle keeps it whatever 495 does.\n42-\n43-495's side is stated by the text that holds it, and only cited here.\n44-\n45-- **Who writes the tests: under trial.**\n46-  - The cycle: the session that writes the code writes its tests first, and the tool replays the\n47-    test-only commit to see it fail.\n48-  - 495: a separate preparation intervention writes the tests before any candidate exists, and the\n49-    kernel judges them on the bare reference and freezes them with the protocol at G2\n50-    (`src/application/phases/prepare.ts`, `D-14`).\n51-  - At stake: the separation keeps tests from being cut to fit the code. It costs a frozen test that\n52:    no implementation can satisfy, which today stops the change (`specs/bugs/registry.yaml`).\n53-- **Who reviews: under trial.**\n54-  - The cycle: every story gets two model reviewers, for two rounds, and a promise the code does not\n55-    keep blocks it.\n56-  - 495: a review is a role the frozen protocol may require (`required_reviews`, empty by default).\n57-    When one is required, a rejection or a blocking finding fails G5 (`src/domain/gates/g5.ts`).\n58-  - At stake: whether systematic review earns what it costs.\n59-- **Who accepts: under trial.**\n60-  - The cycle: the owner accepts or names a gap. In an unattended run, a fresh model session decides\n61-    in the owner's place, and the record says `origine: automate`.\n62-  - 495: G5 combines evidence, reviews and human decisions deterministically against the frozen\n63-    protocol. Acceptance follows from G5 by default, or from an IH-10 decisi