{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MoR5Qm2iKY8qMaNhbiXTSJ","type":"tool_result","content":"## Git and commits\n\nOne branch per story or per fix cycle. Commit message: `<type>: <description>`, one line, in English,\nwhatever the language of the session or of the file changed; the types are `feat`, `fix`,\n`refactor`, `docs`, `test`, `chore`, `perf`, `ci`. The message states the resulting behaviour, never\nthe process that produced it: no work-package reference, no review round, no attribution, no\n`Co-Authored-By`. Nothing is pushed by an automaton; `gh` for every GitHub operation, never the API\ndirectly; no issue is created by an automaton.\n\n## What is written by hand, and what is observed\nbugs:\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-09-27T170100\n    date: \"2026-09-27\"\n    title: \"G1 accepts a mandatory requirement's satisfied_by_reference claim without evidence, before G2/G5 are known to catch a false one\"\n    severity: medium\n    priority: p2\n    scope: domain\n    what_happened: >\n      A material answer bound to a mandatory requirement passes G1 (`gateG1`, `src/domain/change/decide.ts`)\n      as soon as the requirement is named, is mandatory, and the binding is observable — G1 never reads\n      `satisfied_by_reference`. A specification report can mark a brand-new, never-implemented\n      requirement `satisfied_by_reference: true` (a claim reserved for behaviour the project already\n      honours, per the `specify` role's own instructions) and still clear G1 unchallenged.\n    what_expected: >\n      A material answer resolved only by a false `satisfied_by_reference` claim should not clear a gate\n      on the model's word alone — some control downstream must be known to catch it, or G1 itself must\n      refuse it.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"Write a specification report whose mandatory requirement is marked `satisfied_by_reference: true` for behaviour the reference tree does not have, with a material answer bound to it.\"\n      - \"Advance the change through G1: `gateG1` names no reason and passes.\"\n      - \"Whether `preparation.ts`'s discrimination against the reference (feeding G2) or G5's evidence review would still catch the false claim before integration was not traced in this pass.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      `satisfied_by_reference` is read by `preparation.ts` (`~line 116`) to flag a requirement as\n      `undiscriminated` when the reference tree fails to exercise it, feeding a later gate's evidence —\n      but `gateG1` (`~decide.ts:505-515`) never consults it before passing the requirements document, so\n      a false claim is not refused at the gate meant to bind an answer to a real obligation; whether it\n      is refused later depends on control paths this pass did not trace to a verdict.\n    evidence: >\n      Read during the round-1 review of e01s03 (`specs/verifications/e01s03-relecture.md`, P2):\n      `gateG1`'s mandatory-answer loop names no check on `satisfied_by_reference`; `preparation.ts:116-126`\n      is the only reader found, and it is proved only by the tests that already pass, not by a new\n      failing one exercising a false claim. G2 and G5's own handling of the case was not traced.\n    reproduced: >\n      Reproduced on 2026-10-01 with a scripted agent on the reference npm target (`npm run campagne -- npm`).\n      A specification report whose only mandatory requirement asks for a function the tree does not have,\n      marked `satisfied_by_reference: true`, and an implementation that only appends a comment, pass G0 to G5\n      (unit, coverage and mutation PASS, `lastFit` absent from the candidate): `diagnoseControlCapability`\n      reports no undiscriminated requirement while the reference suite executes a test, so no failing test is\n      prepared, no arbitration is asked, and every control stays green on the reference and on the candidate.\n      The same report with `satisfied_by_reference: false` stops at \"no test can judge R1\" and waits for the\n    title: \"cycle mesures reads the companion only for a story given under the exact label tla, so a formal profile given under another label is measured and recommended\"\n    severity: low\n    priority: p3\n    scope: cycle\n    what_happened: >\n      `verifierProfil` (`cycle/src/mesures.ts`) refuses a story whose companion keeps no `modele-d-etats` with a\n      manifest only when the label before `=` is exactly `tla` (`PROFIL_A_ETATS`). A story given as `formel=<story>` —\n      the label the recipe that found the gap used — or as `TLA=<story>` is still measured under that label, and the\n      bilan writes a recommendation for it although the story's companion keeps only `exemples`.\n    what_expected: >\n      A bilan never recommends a formal means on a story whose companion keeps none, whatever label the story is given\n      under: the profile is read in the companion, not in the label.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"Run `npm run cycle -- mesures simple=<s1> formel=<s2>` with a story `<s2>` whose companion keeps only `exemples`: the bilan measures it and recommends for `formel`.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      The label is matched against the one name `tla` that `specs/experiments/e38/README.md` fixes for the\n      state-machine profile; any other label is taken as written, with no check of what the story was verified with.\n    evidence: >\n      Introduced by the branch e38s13, which adds the companion check for the state-machine profile. Read in\n      `cycle/src/mesures.ts` during the round-1 review; the promise holds for the label the README names.\n    risk_level: low\n    follow_ups: >\n      Derive the profile of a story from its companion, or refuse a label `cycle mesures` does not know, rather than\n      checking one label.\n    status: open\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-10-11T040100\n    date: \"2026-10-11\"\n    title: \"A profile whose suite_paths match no .mjs file leaves the coverage witness unrun, so a Node target that adopts the coverage complement cannot freeze its protocol\"\n    severity: medium\n    priority: p2\n    scope: application\n    what_happened: >\n      `withSuitePaths` (`src/application/project-controls.ts`) appends the profile's `suite_paths` to the suite the\n      technology detects. The Node coverage control reads the LCOV report that suite writes, and qualifies on witness\n      tests written as `test/495-covered-witness.test.mjs` and `test/495-uncovered-witness.test.mjs`\n      (`witnessTestLayout`, `src/adapters/stacks/node/project/witness-layout.ts`). Under a profile naming\n      `test/*.test.js` alone, those witnesses are never run: both witnesses give INDETERMINATE (\"no coverage report\n      measures ... src/witness495/covered.mjs\") and the change stops at qualification with CAPABILITY_MISSING\n      \"protocol not frozen\". The stable controller of pi-495 (`specs/experiments/e38/dogfood/config.json`) names\n      `test/*.test.js`, `test/*.test.ts`, `test/v0-pure/**/*.test.ts` and `test/cycle/**/*.test.ts`, none of which\n      matches a `.mjs` witness, so a dogfooding change whose owner adopts the coverage complement would block there.\n    what_expected: >\n      The test files a profile names narrow what the suite runs of the target's tests, never the witnesses 495\n      writes to qualify a control on that suite.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"In `test/v2-kernel/preparation.test.ts`, the test of the frozen unit control under an adopted complement, set `policy: { suite_paths: [\\\"test/*.test.js\\\"] }`: the change ends in qualification, blocked, with the coverage witnesses INDETERMINATE.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      The witnesses' paths are chosen by the technology and the suite's scope by the profile, and nothing reconciles\n      them: qualification runs the frozen command, scope included, on a tree whose witnesses lie outside it.\n    evidence: >\n      Introduced by the branch e38s13, which adds `suite_paths`. Observed while writing the test of the second\n      detection during the round-1 review: with `test/*.test.js` alone the protocol is not frozen, with\n      `test/*.test.mjs` added it is. The dogfooding campaign's answerer does not adopt a complement, so the real\n      campaign does not reach this path.\n    risk_level: medium\n    follow_ups: >\n      Run a control's qualification with the witness files it writes added to the suite's scope, or have the profile's\n      scope apply to the target's tests alone; until then, a profile naming `suite_paths` has to match the witnesses'\n      `.mjs` files.\n    status: open","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5beddbe4-a768-409d-882e-9300e5d3988f","uuid":"7447a688-2acb-4e1b-b801-d5a2ace24316","timestamp":"2026-10-11T03:02:45.578Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## Git and commits\n\nOne branch per story or per fix cycle. Commit message: `<type>: <description>`, one line, in English,\nwhatever the language of the session or of the file changed; the types are `feat`, `fix`,\n`refactor`, `docs`, `test`, `chore`, `perf`, `ci`. The message states the resulting behaviour, never\nthe process that produced it: no work-package reference, no review round, no attribution, no\n`Co-Authored-By`. Nothing is pushed by an automaton; `gh` for every GitHub operation, never the API\ndirectly; no issue is created by an automaton.\n\n## What is written by hand, and what is observed\nbugs:\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-09-27T170100\n    date: \"2026-09-27\"\n    title: \"G1 accepts a mandatory requirement's satisfied_by_reference claim without evidence, before G2/G5 are known to catch a false one\"\n    severity: medium\n    priority: p2\n    scope: domain\n    what_happened: >\n      A material answer bound to a mandatory requirement passes G1 (`gateG1`, `src/domain/change/decide.ts`)\n      as soon as the requirement is named, is mandatory, and the binding is observable — G1 never reads\n      `satisfied_by_reference`. A specification report can mark a bran